      "content": "Certainly, strong arguments suggest that the decline of religious authority can lead to a \"morality vacuum,\" potentially causing social disorganization and an increase in deviance.\n\nHowever, a critical perspective argues that the very power of religious institutions can fuel conflict, particularly when doctrines are rigid and exclusive of certain groups.\n\nThis ideological polarization can lead to \"in-group vs. out-group\" bias, promoting social division and political extremism rather than consensus.\n\nUltimately, the relationship is complex, with religious belief sometimes promoting strong social trust and other times promoting rigid social conflict.",
